    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The decrease in costs associated with genetic sequencing and the recent growth in large-scale data collection have resulted in the increasing prevalence of genome-wide association studies.  The United Kingdom Biobank is an organization that has collected numerous pieces of phenotypic data from approximately 500,000 participants, which includes time series data from a sub-maximal exercise test. In conjunction with their genetic sequencing data, our study extracted two direct measures of physical fitness, which were then used as fitness scores in two GWAS. Our results identify 33 distinct genomic regions associated with physical fitness, which have upregulated genes in the muscle and heart tissues and have significant overlaps with gene sets responsible for muscular and cardiac function and development. In addition, our study finds five novel regions that have not been associated with previous GWAS. These loci are associated with metabolic and immune pathways and include mappings to the THRB, CADM2, and DLEU2 genes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hsu, David and Ashley, Euan and Bergmann, Dominique and Skotheim, Jan. (2018). Fitness genome-wide association study identifies five novel loci that are related to metabolic and immune pathways. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/cj807rx3233",
            "type": "preferred citation",
